## 🧠 Key concepts in plain terms

| Term | Plain meaning |
| --- | --- |
| **Database** | The biggest container. Yours is `MEDICAID`. |
| **Schema** | A folder inside the database. Reference tables go in `REF`. |
| **Table** | A single dataset (rows and columns), like `NPPES_RAW`. |
| **Full address** | `DATABASE.SCHEMA.TABLE` — a folder path, e.g. `MEDICAID.REF.NPPES_RAW`. |
| **Stage** | A holding area you upload files into *before* loading them into a table. |
| **File format** | The rules for reading a CSV — where quotes are, whether there's a header, etc. |
| **`INFER_SCHEMA`** | Snowflake reads the header row and builds all the columns for you. |
| **`COPY INTO`** | The command that actually loads the staged files into a table. |
| **`MATCH_BY_COLUMN_NAME`** | Lines up file columns to table columns **by name**, so order doesn't matter. |
| **NPI** | A provider's 10-digit national ID number. |
| **Taxonomy code** | A 10-character code for a specialty (e.g. `207Q00000X` = Family Medicine). |

> [!NOTE]
> **Why split the file?** The browser upload has a **250 MB** limit per file. The raw CSV is ~10 GB, so it must be broken into pieces.
>
> **Why gzip?** Gzip shrinks each part from ~240 MB to ~22 MB. Snowflake reads gzip natively, so uploads are ~10× smaller and faster with zero downside.

