## 2️⃣ Step 2 — Split + zip on your PC

**Goal:** turn the one giant CSV into many small `.csv.gz` parts, each with the header row, ready to upload.

> [!WARNING]
> **Do this in a normal local folder** (e.g. `Downloads`), **not** in OneDrive. OneDrive tries to sync every part as it's written, which throttles the whole job and locks files.

### 2a. Split the file

Reads the big CSV line by line and writes ~240 MB parts, copying the **header row into every part**. (NPPES puts one record per line, so splitting on line boundaries is safe.)

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The header **must** be in every part. Snowflake reads the first line of *each* file as the header — if a part is missing it, that part's first real row gets dropped and the columns won't line up.

```powershell
# ============================================================
#  NPPES splitter — makes ~240 MB parts, header in each part
# ============================================================

# ---- CONFIG: edit these three paths ----
$src      = "C:\path\to\npidata_pfile.csv"                 # the big NPPES CSV
$outDir   = "C:\Users\<you>\Downloads\NPPES_split250"      # local, NOT OneDrive
$maxBytes = 240MB                                          # stay under the 250 MB limit

# ---- Setup ----
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path $outDir | Out-Null

$reader = [System.IO.StreamReader]::new($src)
$header = $reader.ReadLine()                     # first line = the header (BOM auto-stripped on read)
$enc    = [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false)  # write WITHOUT a byte-order mark

$part   = 0
$writer = $null
$bytes  = 0

# ---- Main loop ----
$writer = [System.IO.StreamWriter]::new(
            (Join-Path $outDir ("NPPES_part{0:D3}.csv" -f (++$part))), $false, $enc)
$writer.WriteLine($header)

while (($line = $reader.ReadLine()) -ne $null) {
    if ($bytes -ge $maxBytes) {                 # current part is full → start a new one
        $writer.Close()
        $writer = [System.IO.StreamWriter]::new(
                    (Join-Path $outDir ("NPPES_part{0:D3}.csv" -f (++$part))), $false, $enc)
        $writer.WriteLine($header)              # header goes in EVERY part
        $bytes = 0
    }
    $writer.WriteLine($line)
    $bytes += $enc.GetByteCount($line) + 2      # +2 for the line ending
}

$writer.Close()
$reader.Close()

Write-Host "Done. Created $part parts in $outDir"
```

**Result:** `NPPES_part001.csv`, `NPPES_part002.csv`, … each ~240 MB with a header row.

### 2b. Gzip each part, then delete the raw CSVs

```powershell
$outDir   = "C:\Users\<you>\Downloads\NPPES_split250"
$sevenZip = "C:\Program Files\7-Zip\7z.exe"

Get-ChildItem $outDir -Filter *.csv | ForEach-Object {
    Start-Process -FilePath $sevenZip `
        -ArgumentList "a","-tgzip","$($_.FullName).gz","$($_.FullName)" `
        -Wait -NoNewWindow                       # -Wait = finish before the next one (avoids file locks)
    Remove-Item $_.FullName -Force               # delete the raw .csv once it's zipped
}
```

> [!CAUTION]
> **Use 7-Zip, not `Compress-Archive`.** `Compress-Archive` makes a `.zip`, which Snowflake won't auto-detect. You need `.gz` (gzip).

### 2c. If some `.csv` didn't get zipped (locked-file recovery)

If the delete step errors with *"being used by another process"* (antivirus or Explorer preview holding a file), some raw `.csv` files may be left behind. Run this after the main job finishes — it gzips only the ones missing a `.gz`, then deletes the raw files:

```powershell
cd "C:\Users\<you>\Downloads\NPPES_split250"

Get-ChildItem *.csv | Where-Object { -not (Test-Path "$($_.FullName).gz") } | ForEach-Object {
    Start-Process -FilePath "C:\Program Files\7-Zip\7z.exe" `
        -ArgumentList "a","-tgzip","$($_.FullName).gz","$($_.FullName)" `
        -Wait -NoNewWindow
}
Remove-Item *.csv -Force
```

### 2d. Confirm you're left with only `.gz`

```powershell
Get-ChildItem "$outDir\*.csv.gz" | Measure-Object   # Count = your number of parts
Get-ChildItem "$outDir\*.csv"    | Measure-Object   # Count = 0
```

