## 5️⃣ Step 5 — Build the wide table

No hand-typing. Snowflake reads the header of a staged file and builds all 330 columns for you.

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE MEDICAID.REF.NPPES_RAW
USING TEMPLATE (
  SELECT ARRAY_AGG(OBJECT_CONSTRUCT(*))
  FROM TABLE(
    INFER_SCHEMA(
      LOCATION    => '@MEDICAID.REF.NPPES_STAGE',
      FILE_FORMAT => 'MEDICAID.REF.NPPES_CSV'
    )
  )
);
```

**What this does:** creates an empty `NPPES_RAW` shaped exactly like the file — all 330 columns, named from the header. No rows yet.

**Peek at the columns (optional):**

```sql
DESCRIBE TABLE MEDICAID.REF.NPPES_RAW;
```

Check the first column reads as a clean `NPI` (no hidden characters in front of it).

---

## 6️⃣ Step 6 — Load the data

```sql
COPY INTO MEDICAID.REF.NPPES_RAW
FROM @MEDICAID.REF.NPPES_STAGE
FILE_FORMAT = (FORMAT_NAME = 'MEDICAID.REF.NPPES_CSV')
MATCH_BY_COLUMN_NAME = CASE_INSENSITIVE   -- match file columns to table columns by name
ON_ERROR = CONTINUE;                      -- skip a bad row instead of aborting the whole load
```

**What you'll see:** one result row **per file**. Every file should say status **`LOADED`** with `errors_seen = 0`.

**Confirm the row count:**

```sql
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM MEDICAID.REF.NPPES_RAW;   -- expect roughly 9,600,000
```

> [!NOTE]
> This takes a few minutes on a running warehouse. If it seems stuck, make sure a warehouse is actually running — `COPY` needs compute.

